counterexample:
Mapping into typed domain variants or wrappers that preserve the required source/context is legitimate:
```rust
fn load_config() -> Result<Config, ConfigError> {
    let text = std::fs::read_to_string("config.toml").map_err(ConfigError::Io)?;
    toml::from_str(&text).map_err(ConfigError::Format)
}
```
Deliberate erasure at a final presentation boundary or in a health predicate is acceptable when no caller needs different programmatic handling:
```rust
fn is_system_healthy() -> bool {
    check_db().is_ok() && check_cache().is_ok()
}
```
A dynamic error container (e.g. `Box<dyn Error>`, `anyhow::Error`) that retains useful source/downcast information is not inherently a flat string.

fix:
Define a composite error enum or structured error type with meaningful stage/domain variants that preserve the underlying causes. Map subsystem errors into those variants at integration points, implement `Display` and `std::error::Error` (including `source()` chaining), and convert to user-facing text only after the required classification and recovery decisions have been made.
```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
pub enum PaymentError {
    Validation(ValidationError),
    Network(reqwest::Error),
}

impl std::fmt::Display for PaymentError {
    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {
        match self {
            PaymentError::Validation(e) => write!(f, "order validation failed: {e}"),
            PaymentError::Network(e) => write!(f, "card charge failed: {e}"),
        }
    }
}

impl std::error::Error for PaymentError {
    fn source(&self) -> Option<&(dyn std::error::Error + 'static)> {
        match self {
            PaymentError::Validation(e) => Some(e),
            PaymentError::Network(e) => Some(e),
        }
    }
}

fn process_payment(order_id: u64) -> Result<(), PaymentError> {
    validate_order(order_id).map_err(PaymentError::Validation)?;
    charge_card(order_id).map_err(PaymentError::Network)?;
    Ok(())
}
```
